## What you can do
With this project, you can give a llm a hand to the outside world. no complex protocols and standards, just simple thing a model actually needs to use the hand

## Chat with a local Llama server

Start a llama.cpp server with its OpenAI-compatible API listening on port 8080, then run `npm start` or `node index.js`. `api.js` and `chat.js` export API and chat-with-tools classes without starting the app. `index.js` connects `LlamaAPI`, `LlamaChatWithTools`, and the tool list to run the default chat. When the model calls a tool, the chat class runs it, sends the result back to the model, and continues until the model responds without another tool call. Conversation history is kept until you enter `/exit` or `/quit`.

## Tool-calling classes

`ChatWithTools`, `CompatibilityChatWithTools`, `ToolSupportingModelChatWithTools`, `LlamaChatWithTools`, and `OllamaChatWithTools` are exported from `chat.js`. The API classes are exported from `api.js`. To select a different combination, change the imports and constructor calls in `index.js`, for example `new OllamaChatWithTools(new OllamaAPI(), tools)`.

`CompatibilityChatWithTools` includes tool definitions and calling instructions in the system prompt, parses tool calls from the response text, and sends results as `user` messages. `ToolSupportingModelChatWithTools` sends tool definitions using the OpenAI-compatible native tool-calling API and sends tool results as `tool` messages. `LlamaChatWithTools` sends loaded images as image content in the corresponding tool result. `OllamaChatWithTools` sends tool results first, then sends loaded images in a separate `user` message after the assistant responds to the tool result.

The `load_image` tool reads PNG, JPEG, GIF, or WebP files from disk. Image messages use the OpenAI-compatible `image_url` content format, and the connected model must support vision. `OllamaChatWithTools` sends the image as a user message; the llama.cpp `LlamaChatWithTools` class includes it in the tool result.

The llama.cpp endpoint defaults to `http://localhost:8080/v1/chat/completions`; set `LLAMA_URL` to override it. Ollama uses `http://localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions` by default, includes `ministral-3:3b` as the request model, and can be configured with `OLLAMA_URL` and `OLLAMA_MODEL`.